<h2>A fifteen-year arc, ending where it began</h2>
<p>The timeline tells its own story. A Share Purchase Agreement in December 2010; termination in August 2012; invocation of arbitration in June 2013; a reminder in July 2014; and then, in December 2014, the claimant&rsquo;s unilateral nomination of a sole arbitrator when the contractual mechanism did not move. Five years later came an award of Rs 77 crore in the claimant&rsquo;s favour. Six years after that, the Single Judge set the award aside under Section 34 — not on merits, but because the tribunal itself had no lawful existence. The Division Bench has now closed the circle, finding &ldquo;no case made out for interference.&rdquo;</p>

<h2>The reasoning, step by step</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The appointment was void ab initio.</strong> Clause 20.4 of the SPA prescribed how the tribunal was to be constituted. A unilateral nomination outside that clause, and outside Section 11, produced an arbitrator with no jurisdiction at all.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Protest is not ratification.</strong> The respondent&rsquo;s participation in the proceedings under protest did not amount to acceptance of the arbitrator&rsquo;s authority.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>No waiver under Section 4.</strong> Waiver presupposes a curable procedural lapse; a void constitution of the tribunal is not one.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Section 12(5) does not rescue it.</strong> The 2015 Amendment&rsquo;s ineligibility regime could not retrospectively validate an appointment that was bad for a different and more basic reason.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Section 37 is not a second Section 34.</strong> Grounds not urged before the Single Judge could not be raised for the first time in appeal.</div>
</div>

<h2>Where this sits in the law on unilateral appointments</h2>
<p>The decision belongs to the now-settled line of authority, culminating in the Constitution Bench&rsquo;s ruling on unilateral arbitrator appointments, that party autonomy in constituting a tribunal means the <em>agreed</em> mechanism — and where it fails, the court-appointment route. What this judgment adds is temporal breadth: the appointment here predated the 2015 Amendment, and the Bench reached the same result on first principles of contract and Section 11, without needing the Seventh Schedule at all.</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>If the appointment mechanism stalls</th><th>Consequence</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Apply to court under Section 11</td><td>Tribunal validly constituted; award enforceable</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Appoint your own arbitrator unilaterally</td><td>Appointment void ab initio; award liable to be set aside, whatever its merits — years of proceedings lost</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="note">This report summarises a judgment of the High Court of Delhi dated 1 October 2026 for general information. It is not legal advice.</div>
